    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis explores the integration of advanced electron microscopy techniques with machine learning to enhance the characterization and analysis of shale and sandstone samples at the nanoscale. Understanding fluid dynamics within these rock formations is critical for various applications, including oil and gas recovery, \\(CO_2\\) storage, and subsurface fluid transport. The study utilizes both Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM) and Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) to achieve high-resolution imaging of pore networks and mineral fabric. These techniques provide detailed insights into the microstructural properties of geological samples, overcoming traditional imaging limitations.\r\n\r\nTo improve outcomes from destructive imaging techniques, such as Focused Ion Beam Scanning Electron Microscopy (FIB-SEM), this research implements Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) to predict intermediate images between successive slices. This approach significantly improves the accuracy and continuity of 3D reconstructions, with Structural Similarity Index (SSIM) values demonstrating the model’s effectiveness. Additionally, data augmentation strategies were employed to diversify training datasets, further enhancing the robustness of the GAN models.\r\n\r\nThe thesis also introduces automated workflows for pore segmentation using ilastik and 3D reconstruction via PerGeos, enabling the calculation of critical petrophysical properties such as porosity and permeability. These methodologies provide a comprehensive framework for understanding the complex pore networks within shale and sandstone, ultimately contributing to more efficient and accurate subsurface fluid modeling.\r\n\r\nWhile the research presented here lays a solid foundation, it also highlights areas for future exploration, including the refinement of GAN models, the integration of multimodal data, and the potential to reduce reliance on destructive imaging methods. By advancing these techniques, this thesis contributes to the field of digital rock physics, offering new tools and insights for the characterization of geological materials.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Li, H. (2024). Electron Microscopy and Image Analysis of Nanoporous Materials. Version 1. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/kd437yb3029/version/1. https://doi.org/10.25740/kd437yb3029.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
